=== Setting Up a Development Environment ===

Use conda with the included dev-environment.yml file. It builds into an untracked env/
directory inside the repository, which .gitignore already covers.

$ conda env create -f dev-environment.yml --prefix=$PWD/env
$ conda activate $PWD/env

Or run individual commands without activating:

$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest

This environment deliberately does NOT include breseq. The pre-release breseq builds that
CNery targets declare cnery-prerelease as a hard dependency, so installing breseq here would
place a packaged copy of CNery into the environment's own site-packages, shadowing your
working tree anywhere pyproject.toml's pythonpath=["src"] does not reach. Nothing in the test
suite shells out to breseq; tests read coverage tables that were generated ahead of time.

If you do need breseq for generating new test data, install it separately. See the
"Generating a coverage table" section of README.md.

=== Running Tests ===

Run pytest from the repository root. pyproject.toml supplies testpaths, pythonpath=["src"],
and the default marker filter, so no install step or PYTHONPATH fiddling is needed.

Run everything. This is the default. On a cold cache it downloads ~105 MB of authentic
datasets and takes about a minute; afterwards it is roughly a minute from cache:
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest

Run only the synthetic tier. Fast, offline, a few seconds -- the inner loop while editing:
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest -m synthetic

Run only the authentic tier:
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest -m authentic

Run one file, one test, or by name:
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest tests/test_hmm.py
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest tests/test_utils.py::TestFindNearest::test_exact_match
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest -k gc_correction

=== The Two Test Tiers ===

The suite is split into two tiers, and which one a test belongs in is a deliberate choice.
Both run by default; the markers exist to let you opt *out*, not in.

SYNTHETIC TIER. DataFrames built in tests/conftest.py. Fast, hermetic, no network. Most tests
belong here, and this tier must stay offline: `-m synthetic` should never download anything or
depend on an external service.

AUTHENTIC TIER. Real breseq coverage tables fetched from GitHub Releases. Use it only when
synthetic data genuinely cannot exercise what you are testing -- real coordinate gaps, real
repeat regions, real per-read-group columns, the actual on-disk file schema.

Tests are marked with @pytest.mark.authentic. You do NOT mark synthetic tests: conftest.py
applies the `synthetic` marker automatically to anything not marked authentic, so the two
markers stay exhaustive and mutually exclusive and a new test cannot land in neither tier.

The default deliberately includes the authentic tier. An opt-in tier is one people forget, and
real-data coverage then lapses silently. The cost is that a cold-cache run is slow, which is
what `-m synthetic` is for.

IMPORTANT: an unfetchable dataset skips rather than fails, so offline work stays possible.
That means a default run without network access reports passes and skips together. Check for
skips instead of reading green as proof everything ran.

=== Writing Tests in the Default Tier ===

Reuse the fixtures in tests/conftest.py rather than hand-rolling DataFrames. They are staged
to match the pipeline's column contract at each stage, so a change to that contract surfaces
in one place instead of in every test:

  windowed_flat, windowed_with_deletion, windowed_with_amplification
                          -- post-preprocess frames (read_count_cov, norm_raw_cov, gc_percent)
  gc_corrected_flat       -- adds gc_corr_norm_cov, gc_corr_fact
  otr_corrected_flat      -- adds otr_gc_corr_norm_cov, otr_gc_corr_fact
  single_fasta            -- a one-record FASTA on disk

The writer functions assume their output subdirectories already exist. Any test reaching
otr_correction, run_HMM, or a plot function must create CNV_plt/, CNV_csv/, GC_bias/ and
OTR_corr/ under tmp_path first -- see _ensure_dirs in tests/test_integration.py.

For anything invoking breseq, patch subprocess.run with a side effect that writes a fake
table, as tests/test_bam2cov_io.py does. Do not assume a fixed footer length: breseq writes
four commented summary lines by default, one more under --show-average, three per read group,
and none at all in its own 08_mutation_identification output. TestFooterHandling covers each
of those shapes.

Make assertions match the test name. A test called test_deletion_yields_cn0 that only asserts
the result is not None passes forever and protects nothing.

=== Adding an Authentic Dataset ===

Datasets are published as GitHub Release assets, one release per dataset version, tagged
testdata-<name>-v<N> and carrying a single <name>.tar.gz. Adding or revising one touches only
its own entry in tests/data/registry.json; no other dataset is regenerated or re-uploaded.

Stage a folder containing what CNery reads -- the reference FASTA and one coverage table per
sequence, named <seq_id>.coverage.tsv. Do NOT include the BAM: CNery reads coverage tables
directly through --coverage-dir and never opens it, so shipping it would multiply the download
size for nothing.

Build and inspect without uploading:
$ python tests/data/add_testdata.py <folder> --name <name> --description "..."

The first run drops a dataset.json provenance stub into the folder. FILL IT IN before
publishing: breseq version and command line, source reads, reference accession, what the
dataset exercises, and any known limitations. Authentic data without provenance stops being
reproducible the moment whoever generated it moves on, and the release tag is immutable, so
correcting provenance afterwards means cutting a new version.

Publish, then paste the printed entry into tests/data/registry.json:
$ python tests/data/add_testdata.py <folder> --name <name> --publish

--version defaults to one past the highest already published, checking both the GitHub
releases and the registry, which can disagree. Reusing a published version is refused, and
that check runs before the archive is built so a clash surfaces on a dry run.

Archives are built reproducibly (gzip and tar mtimes zeroed), so rebuilding the same folder
gives the same sha256 and a published asset can be independently verified. The sha256 in the
registry -- not the tag -- is what pins dataset identity: an asset replaced in place fails the
hash check loudly rather than silently changing what the tests measure. Never overwrite a
published asset; publish a new version instead.

Downloads are cached in pooch's cache directory. Set CNERY_TESTDATA_DIR to relocate it, for
example to share one cache between checkouts or to keep large tables off a small disk.

The published datasets, chosen to differ where it matters rather than to accumulate more of
the same:

  ltee_ara_p1_50k_shift   24 MB   0 read groups, 10 cols,  4-line footer, permuted reference
  ltee_ara_m3_32k_2rg     48 MB   2 read groups, 26 cols, 10-line footer, CN up to 10
  ltee_ara_m3_38k         37 MB   1 read group,  18 cols,  7-line footer, 108 kb CN=2 region

The three footer lengths matter: none is 4, so together they are real regression coverage for
the prefix-based footer stripping that replaced skipfooter=4. A fixed skip of four lines would
silently delete 6 and 3 real data rows from the 32k and 38k tables respectively.

When adding a dataset, prefer one that covers something none of the existing ones do. Another
sample with the same shape costs download time and adds little.

=== Golden Files ===

Expected outputs live in tests/data/expected/ and are compared exactly. Heavy inputs live in
Releases; expected outputs stay in the repository, because a behavior change should show up as
a reviewable diff rather than being hidden inside a release asset.

Keep goldens small and structural. The copy-number golden is the ~25-row break-points segment
table, not the thousands of per-window rows: it is the same information for review purposes
and it is actually readable in a diff.

Goldens are only meaningful at fixed window parameters. The tests pin -w and -s; changing them
invalidates the goldens.

After an intended behavior change, refresh and review:
$ conda run -p $PWD/env pytest -m authentic --regenerate-goldens
$ git diff tests/data/expected/

Regenerated tests report as SKIPPED, not passed -- a run that rewrote its own expectations has
verified nothing. Always read the diff before committing it. If a golden changed and you
cannot explain why, that is a bug, not a golden that needs updating.

Avoid float-exact assertions on corrected coverage. LOWESS and scipy.optimize.minimize drift
across library versions; prefer structural facts (segment boundaries, copy-number states,
origin/terminus windows) or numpy.testing.assert_allclose with an explicit tolerance.

=== Recording Known-Bad Behavior ===

Some tests deliberately record what the code currently does rather than what it should do, as
a tripwire while an area is under development. TestOriginTerminus in
tests/test_authentic_ltee.py is the current example: the LTEE dataset's rotated reference was
meant to exercise the otr_fit branch where the origin index is below the terminus index, but
does not reach it, because the median-filter seed places the two about 6% of the genome apart,
below the 35% floor in otr_fit.

When you do this, say so explicitly in the test docstring, and write the assertions so they
fail if the behavior changes in either direction. A test that quietly encodes a bug as correct
is worse than no test.
